# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Determining Rank — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Chapter Three — Determining Rank'
HEAD = '''Fifteen passages. The hardest chapter in the book to read, and the least like prose: it has almost no sentences, only names. “Accord with heaven, make wealth abundant, gladden the multitude, take the advantage of the ground, put weapons first — these are called the five considerations.” “Not submitting, not trusting, not in concord, slack, doubtful, weary, cowed, at cross purposes, shrunken, worn out, unruly, collapsing, sluggish — these are called the afflictions of war.” It is a run of terms first named and then defined, the nearest thing to a glossary in early Chinese military writing, and that is exactly why it is hard: every commentator since the Ming has admitted that the chapter has lost slips and has them out of order, and where it cannot be construed the commentators can do no more than set out the possibilities. This page does not force such places, and does not emend them. Two things are worth carrying away: “the general is the body, the soldiers are the limbs, the squads are the fingers and toes” — an army seen as one body; and the second half of the way to destroy an evil omen, “feed his excess, take away what he loves, I from without and he from within,” which says everything about subversion in ten characters. The title is simply the first two words of the chapter, as all the titles in this book are.'''

PARA = {
 'p3-1': 'In war: fix the order of rank; make merit and fault plain; gather in the wandering knights; publish the instructions and orders; question the multitude; seek out their skills; measure your deliberations and exhaust the possibilities; sort out what is doubtful and probe what is suspect; husband strength and look for ingenuity; act along the movement of men’s hearts. (Every one of the ten is a thing done before the fighting starts — nothing here is about contact. The whole chapter is like this.)',
 'p3-2': 'In war: make the multitude firm; observe the ground’s advantage; put disorder in order; control advance and halt; make men submit to what is right; bring them to a sense of shame; keep the code brief; keep punishments few. Small offences are nevertheless put to death; when the small offences are mastered, the great ones cease of themselves. (“Keep punishments few” and “small offences are put to death” look contradictory and are not: few punishments, and every one of the few carried out.)',
 'p3-3': 'Accord with heaven, make wealth abundant, gladden the multitude, take the advantage of the ground, put weapons first: these are called the five considerations. To accord with heaven is to keep to the season; to make wealth abundant is to take it from the enemy; to gladden the multitude is to encourage them to comply; to take the advantage of the ground is to hold the defiles and the difficult places; to put weapons first is that bow and arrow are for repelling, pole-arm and spear for holding, dagger-axe and halberd for support. Each of the five weapons has its use: the long protect the short, the short come to the rescue of the long. Fight by turns and you last; fight all at once and you are strong. See what the enemy has and match it — this is called equalling him.',
 'p3-4': 'The commander stands firm and urges compliance, and moves only when he has seen the enemy. The general’s mind is a mind; the mind of the multitude is a mind; horses, oxen, chariots and weapons, rested and fed, are strength. Training lies in doing it beforehand; battle lies in restraint. The general is the body, the soldiers are the limbs, the squads are the fingers and toes. (The last sentence is the best image in the book: an army is one body, and the general is not its owner but the body itself.)',
 'p3-5': 'In war, the thing is wisdom; in close fighting, courage; in formation, skill. Use what you wish to use, do what you are able to do, discard what you do not wish and cannot do — and with the enemy do the reverse. (The Siku text reads “in war the thing is <i>expedience</i>.” “With the enemy do the reverse” — make him use what he does not wish to use and attempt what he cannot do. One clause turns the whole passage inside out, and it is the sharpest thing in the chapter.)',
 'p3-6': 'In war there is what comes of heaven, what comes of wealth, and what comes of good management. Not to let the day slip, to have a favourable augury and to move under cover — this is called having heaven. For the multitude to have stores, and for advantages to grow out of what they have — this is called having wealth. For the men to be practised in formation and the equipment to be serviceable, and for supplies to have been got in beforehand — this is called having good management. For every man to exert himself up to his charge — this is called a willing people. A great army depends on firmness; great strength on close ordering; to be equal to affairs with a light hand, and to meet the unexpected on sight — this is called being prepared in advance. Light chariots and light infantry, with bow and arrow firm in defence — this is called a great army. Close, still, with strength held inside — this is called a firm formation. To advance and withdraw on that footing — this is called great strength. When those above have leisure to train the men — this is called a close-ordered formation. When everything has its charge — this is called being equal to affairs. To distinguish things on that footing — this is called simple management. (“Close ordering” here means density, not vexation. The passage first names six things and then defines each of them backwards, alternating “A, B, C — this is called X” with “X: A, B, C,” a form rarely met with in pre-Qin prose. The Siku text repeats “equal to affairs” where the base text has “distinguish things.”)',
 'p3-7': 'Weigh the numbers, take account of the ground, take account of the enemy, and order the formation. Attack, battle, defence, advance, withdrawal, halt, front and rear in order, chariots and foot supporting one another — these are called the triad of war. Not submitting, not trusting, not in concord; slack, doubtful, weary, cowed, at cross purposes, shrunken, worn out, unruly, collapsing, sluggish — these are called the afflictions of war. Arrogance upon arrogance, fear upon fear, groaning and idleness, apprehension and dread, regret at every turn — these are called ruin and breakage. Great and small, hard and soft, threes and fives, many and few, all things that come in pairs — these are called the balances of war. (The Sibu congkan text writes two near-identical graphs for “at cross purposes”; both witnesses write a variant for “idleness,” and both read “these are <i>called</i> the triad of war” where the base text has a synonym. The Siku text reads “shrunken and vexed” for “shrunken, worn out.” Thirteen words exhaust everything that can go wrong with an army — the longest such list in early Chinese military writing.)',
 'p3-8': 'In war: use spies at a distance, observation close at hand; go by the season, go by resources; prize good faith and hate doubt. Raise troops in righteousness, act in season, treat men with kindness. On sight of the enemy, be still; on sight of disorder, be unhurried; on sight of danger and difficulty, do not forget your own men. In the state be kind and trustworthy; in the army be broad and martial; at the point of the blade be decisive and quick. In the state be harmonious; in the army keep to the law; at the point of the blade be clear-sighted. In the state show goodwill; in the army show squareness; at the point of the blade show good faith. (Three sets of three: in the state, in the army, at the point of the blade — one man in three shapes, and in all three he keeps faith.)',
 'p3-9': 'In formation: on the march keep loose, in battle keep close, and let the weapons be mixed. If the men are well taught, stillness will bring order, and both awe and advantage will be plain. If men keep faith with one another in what is right they will exert themselves; if deliberation yields results they will submit. When they hit the mark at every moment and obey, their order will be in hand. When things are made plain the eye becomes clear; when deliberation is settled the heart becomes strong. Advance and withdraw without hesitation; on sight of the enemy, form no new plan. Let the man to be executed be executed; do not ask his name, and do not change his flag. (“Mixed weapons” follows chapter two, “if weapons are not mixed they are not effective.” The Siku text reads “do not <i>misreport</i> his name.” The last clause is the coldest in the book: where military law is being carried out, no names and no changed colours — a change of colours would set the men doubting.)',
 'p3-10': 'In all undertakings, what is well done lasts; what follows the ancients goes through; when the oath is made plain, men grow strong and the evil omen is destroyed. There are two ways to destroy an evil omen. The first is called rightness: cover him with good faith, stand over him with strength, lay the foundation, unify the shape of the realm, and there is no one who is not glad — this is called using his own men along with yours. The second is called expedience: feed his excess, take away what he loves; I work from without, and let it work from within. (“Feed his excess” is to help him overflow so that he topples. The ten characters of the last clause say everything about subversion; and the way of rightness stands opposite them. This book gives both.)',
 'p3-11': 'First, men; second, uprightness; third, speech; fourth, skill; fifth, fire; sixth, water; seventh, weapons — these are called the seven instruments of government. Honour, gain, shame, death — these are called the four things that hold men. Awe accumulated in the countenance does no more than change men’s minds: all of it is this same way. Only benevolence has kindred; benevolence without good faith turns back and ruins the man himself. Men as men, uprightness as uprightness, speech as speech, fire as fire. (The four reduplicated words at the end have never been satisfactorily explained: on one reading each thing is to be used as what it is; on another, the first pair is a corruption of “employ men.” This page neither emends nor forces a meaning — the chapter’s lost and disordered slips are admitted by every old commentator, and where a passage will not construe, the possibilities are left standing.)',
 'p3-12': 'The way of war: when the spirit of the men has been roused, publish the orders on the strength of it; let them see it in your face and lead them by your words; warn them while they are afraid and set them to work while they want something; move into the enemy’s ground and make its features serve you; assign the tasks by office — this is called the method of war.',
 'p3-13': 'The shape a body of men takes comes from what the multitude asks for. Try to put it into effect by naming it, and be sure to carry it well. If it ought to go forward and does not, take it in hand yourself and lead it; if it goes forward and does go, keep them at it so that they do not forget. Three times, and it has become an article. What is fitting for men to live by is what is called law. (The Siku text reads “the <i>conduct</i> of men” for “the shape a body of men takes.” The last sentence is the best in the chapter: law is not laid on men from outside — it is how men ought to live.)',
 'p3-14': 'The way to govern disorder has seven articles: first, benevolence; second, good faith; third, straightness; fourth, unity; fifth, rightness; sixth, adaptability; seventh, honour. Establishing the code has seven: first, receiving the charge; second, the code itself; third, setting it up; fourth, speed; fifth, regulating the uniforms; sixth, grading the colours; seventh, that the hundred officers should properly wear nothing beyond their station. In an army, to keep the code in one’s own hands is called sole authority; to stand in awe of the law together with those below is called lawfulness. In the army give no ear to small talk and in battle do not scramble for small gains; to have results every day and act in the smallest things — this is called the Way. (“Nothing beyond their station” is dress above one’s rank. The base text omits the word “properly,” which both witnesses have; it has been supplied, and it is the only character added on this page. The sentence about sole authority and lawfulness uses the archaic and the ordinary graph for “law” side by side, as do both old witnesses; it is not a corruption, and has not been regularised. The Siku text reads “seventh, sole authority” for “seventh, honour.”)',
 'p3-15': 'In war, if uprightness will not work, then act by sole authority; if they do not submit, use the law; if they do not trust one another, make them one. If they are slack, stir them; if they are doubtful, change your course; if the men do not trust those above, then carry out what has been ordered and do not go back on it. Such was the government of antiquity. (The Sibu congkan text reads “if uprightness does not <i>tally</i>.” The last clause has never been agreed on; one old reading is “carry out the order that need not be made credible again.” The chapter ends as it began, in articles — from first to last there is not one story and not one name in it.)',
}
